<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, used only when the bundle is signed
  with a Developer ID (build-app.sh passes them with --options runtime).
  An ad-hoc signature does not enable the hardened runtime and ignores them.

  Deliberately short. The program is not sandboxed - it reads the patient
  folders the user points it at, anywhere on the disk - and asking for the
  App Sandbox would break exactly that.

  allow-jit is the one thing a Metal program needs: wgpu compiles the
  shaders of the viewport and of burn's GPU inference backend at run time
  and hands the result to the Metal driver, which maps it executable. Under
  the hardened runtime that fails without this entitlement, and it fails as
  a crash on the first frame rather than as a message.

  If notarisation or a run on a signed build ever needs more, add it here
  rather than dropping --options runtime: an unhardened Developer ID build
  cannot be notarised at all.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
